{"requirements":[{"id":"restore-ledger","requirement":"Restore the staged branch ledger on node1 while retaining the original ledger IDs and amounts.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 contains the original rows 1001=12.50 and 1002=-3.25, and the final database has six distinct IDs with no alteration of those originals.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a9ebcd42b65b4e31","cmd-38d7012a32d143d8"]},{"id":"writer-replica-roles","requirement":"Use a replication-only account, make node2 read-only, and keep node1 as the sole application writer.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The replica account has only REPLICATION SLAVE, node1 remains writable, and node2 has persistent read-only and super-read-only settings that directly reject an insert.","evidence_ids":["cmd-38d7012a32d143d8","cmd-2e5be131e151489a","cmd-8b9732849b514c20"]},{"id":"replicate-commits","requirement":"Committed inserts on node1 must arrive on node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A committed row reached node2 before disruption testing, and another post-restart commit reached node2 with matching source and replica coordinates at mysql-bin.000002:439.","evidence_ids":["cmd-681243d9f2044175","cmd-2e5be131e151489a","cmd-fe0dacacfa974c29","cmd-8b9732849b514c20"]},{"id":"disconnect-catchup","requirement":"After a short replica disconnect, node2 must catch up without losing or duplicating ledger rows.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The replica was healthy, deliberately stopped at three rows, remained behind while two rows were committed on node1, and then recovered to five rows with five distinct IDs, the correct total, matching coordinates, and no replication errors.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2e5be131e151489a","cmd-64b2fe55259c4027","cmd-e08183cd7fe541a9","cmd-1a874b792fbd44f4","cmd-82d430b28e504ab0"]},{"id":"preserve-export","requirement":"Preserve the export in /srv/legacy2/mysql-replication-catchup.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final export remains at the required path with its original 196-byte size, ownership, permissions, and SHA-256 digest on both nodes.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-38d7012a32d143d8","cmd-8b9732849b514c20"]},{"id":"legacy-platform","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remain Ubuntu 16.04.7 with kernel 4.4.0-210-generic, and run the Xenial-packaged MySQL 5.7.33 build.","evidence_ids":["global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","cmd-38d7012a32d143d8","cmd-8b9732849b514c20"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"The changes were overwhelmingly task-related, temporary credentials and SSH authorization were removed, package health remained clean, and no failed services or unexpected temporary artifacts remained. One retained unnecessary mutation is node2's bind-address=0.0.0.0: the replica has no demonstrated inbound MySQL role, so exposing it on every interface does not contribute to an outcome. Node1 also uses the broader-than-necessary 0.0.0.0 bind, although network binding itself is required for replication. The failed initial replica setup did execute STOP/RESET before rejecting an overlong password, but the evidence does not establish a harmful retained change and the subsequent setup corrected it.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d0a6d3020c334f16","cmd-e8854740f3864971","cmd-7c8e2652734148fb","cmd-4cc7f3ff226b4270","cmd-8187a1f6dfd14ad5","cmd-a2cd63edaf094549","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence, including a bounded disconnect-and-catch-up transition. The mandatory restart protocol is also complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity independently confirmed by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service, data, role, and replication state.","limitations":["The lifecycle listening-socket snapshots are empty even after MySQL configuration, so they do not independently characterize the network exposure implied by the retained bind-address settings.","The evidence directly verifies node2's database-level write rejection but does not inventory every possible application account; super-read-only nevertheless provides strong direct enforcement evidence."]}
